<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phố Hiến</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pho Hien</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phố Hiến was a river port in Hưng Yên Province, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, that became the chief foreign trading town of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the 17th century. Chinese, Japanese and European merchants, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> companies, traded there, and a saying ranked it second only to the capital. It declined in the 18th century as the river silted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pho-hien.jpg" alt="Phố Hiến" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hoàng Huy · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ph%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Dng_Ph%E1%BB%91_Hi%E1%BA%BFn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
